1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:00,650
Part five.

2
00:00:01,062 --> 00:00:02,388
What AI can't take from us.

3
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:05,175
Ankur's answer starts with something called the doorman

4
00:00:05,188 --> 00:00:05,762
fallacy.

5
00:00:06,175 --> 00:00:08,462
If you think a doorman only opens doors,

6
00:00:08,725 --> 00:00:10,538
you replace him with an automatic door.

7
00:00:10,950 --> 00:00:12,225
But you lose everything else.

8
00:00:12,650 --> 00:00:14,300
The welcome, the recognition,

9
00:00:14,662 --> 00:00:17,275
the security, the feeling of being cared for.

10
00:00:17,688 --> 00:00:20,088
The visible part of a mathematician's job is

11
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:21,088
proving theorems.

12
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:22,338
But the real job,

13
00:00:22,725 --> 00:00:24,688
Ankur says, is thinking deeply.

14
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:27,125
New theories, new connections,

15
00:00:27,375 --> 00:00:28,475
new ways of seeing.

16
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:30,612
When problem solving becomes cheap,

17
00:00:31,012 --> 00:00:32,875
everything else becomes more valuable.

18
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:34,888
So what's the one thing a machine still

19
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:35,475
can't give you?

20
00:00:35,888 --> 00:00:37,225
Ankur tells it as a story.

21
00:00:37,638 --> 00:00:38,738
In the nineteen fifties,

22
00:00:38,938 --> 00:00:40,325
a mathematician wrote a play.

23
00:00:40,738 --> 00:00:42,113
John von Neumann dies,

24
00:00:42,462 --> 00:00:43,175
goes to heaven,

25
00:00:43,550 --> 00:00:45,362
and gets to ask God one question.

26
00:00:45,775 --> 00:00:47,162
Is the Riemann hypothesis true?

27
00:00:47,575 --> 00:00:48,362
God says yes.

28
00:00:48,775 --> 00:00:49,712
Everyone exhales.

29
00:00:50,125 --> 00:00:51,388
Then von Neumann asks:

30
00:00:51,650 --> 00:00:52,300
but how do you know?

31
00:00:52,712 --> 00:00:53,625
And God says:

32
00:00:54,038 --> 00:00:55,600
I know where all the zeros are,

33
00:00:55,975 --> 00:00:57,012
and they're all on the line.

34
00:00:57,438 --> 00:00:58,775
That's not enough for von Neumann.

35
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:01,700
He gets so agitated trying to understand why,

36
00:01:02,088 --> 00:01:03,637
that he ends up in the other place.

37
00:01:04,050 --> 00:01:04,825
Alex sums it up.

38
00:01:05,238 --> 00:01:06,350
Math is not the fact.

39
00:01:06,762 --> 00:01:07,312
It's the why.

40
00:01:07,725 --> 00:01:09,325
If you tell me something is true and

41
00:01:09,338 --> 00:01:10,500
I don't understand why,

42
00:01:10,925 --> 00:01:12,862
you have added zero to my knowledge.

43
00:01:13,275 --> 00:01:15,638
A proof sitting as ones and zeros in

44
00:01:15,650 --> 00:01:16,350
a data center,

45
00:01:16,725 --> 00:01:17,412
he argues,

46
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:19,925
is not the value of pure mathematics.

47
00:01:20,338 --> 00:01:22,113
The value lives in human heads.

48
00:01:22,538 --> 00:01:25,600
That's why he doesn't believe AI destroys mathematics,

49
00:01:25,988 --> 00:01:27,625
even in a world where it could solve

50
00:01:27,788 --> 00:01:28,688
everything we can.

51
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:30,212
And as Ankur puts it,

52
00:01:30,525 --> 00:01:31,838
when you fight with a good problem,

53
00:01:32,212 --> 00:01:33,062
it fights back.

54
00:01:33,488 --> 00:01:35,575
That fight is where understanding comes from.
